WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.333
Welcome. This class turns a model's
proposed tool call into

2
00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:10.667
a controlled application action. You will
classify tool risk, write

3
00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:16.000
narrow input and result contracts,
separate schema validation from

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.333
authorization, place approval before
impact, prevent duplicate side effects,

5
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:26.667
verify real postconditions, and recover
honestly when a tool outcome

6
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:32.000
is uncertain.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.250
Not every tool deserves the same
authority. Reading a public

8
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:44.500
document differs from reading private
records. Editing a local draft

9
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.750
differs from sending it. Creating a
preview differs from deploying

10
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:57.000
production. Archiving a recoverable object
differs from deleting it permanently.

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.250
Classify each operation by data
sensitivity, target scope, reversibility,

12
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:09.500
external impact, cost, permission change,
and destruction. Give the run

13
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.750
only the operations and resolved resources
it needs. Separate read

14
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:22.000
credentials from write credentials, scope
temporary credentials to one task,

15
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.250
and expire them. Keep secrets outside
prompts and ordinary logs.

16
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:34.500
If a tool exposes arbitrary shell, SQL,
network, or filesystem

17
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.750
access, treat that breadth as a design
defect unless the

18
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:47.000
task genuinely requires it and a stronger
sandbox contains it.

19
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.818
Give each tool one clear purpose. Define
required fields, allowed

20
00:01:53.818 --> 00:02:00.636
values, formats, length and numeric
bounds, and reject unknown fields.

21
00:02:00.636 --> 00:02:07.455
Use stable result variants such as
succeeded, failed, rejected, conflict,

22
00:02:07.455 --> 00:02:14.273
retryable transport failure, and outcome
unknown. Document which variants may

23
00:02:14.273 --> 00:02:21.091
follow a side effect. Generated arguments
must pass application validation

24
00:02:21.091 --> 00:02:27.909
even when a provider reports valid
structured output. A valid

25
00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:34.727
resource identifier can still name the
wrong tenant's object. A

26
00:02:34.727 --> 00:02:41.545
valid amount can still exceed policy. A
valid operation can

27
00:02:41.545 --> 00:02:48.364
still be forbidden for the caller. The
schema improves parsing

28
00:02:48.364 --> 00:02:55.182
and reduces ambiguity; it does not
authenticate the principal, authorize

29
00:02:55.182 --> 00:03:02.000
the object, express user consent, or prove
success.

30
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.909
Suppose an agent can archive a document.
Its proposal contains

31
00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:13.818
resource ID doc one-two-three, operation
archive, and an idempotency key.

32
00:03:13.818 --> 00:03:19.727
The JSON is valid. The executor
authenticates the caller, resolves

33
00:03:19.727 --> 00:03:25.636
the document, and discovers that it
belongs to a different

34
00:03:25.636 --> 00:03:31.545
workspace. The correct result is
authorization denied, with no archive

35
00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:37.455
attempt. Now the caller selects an owned
document, but the

36
00:03:37.455 --> 00:03:43.364
human approval names a different resource.
Reject that mismatch too.

37
00:03:43.364 --> 00:03:49.273
Finally, the exact target and approval
match. The executor records

38
00:03:49.273 --> 00:03:55.182
the idempotency key, performs one archive,
queries the document state,

39
00:03:55.182 --> 00:04:01.091
and returns succeeded with postcondition
evidence. The model never receives

40
00:04:01.091 --> 00:04:07.000
authority merely by producing well-shaped
arguments.

41
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.818
Approval matters only while the reviewer
can still prevent impact.

42
00:04:13.818 --> 00:04:20.636
Ask before sending, purchasing,
publishing, deploying, changing

43
00:04:20.636 --> 00:04:27.455
permission, or destroying. Show the
resolved target, operation, material

44
00:04:27.455 --> 00:04:34.273
arguments, expected effect, cost,
reversibility, and evidence the reviewer

45
00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:41.091
is approving. Bind the approval to that
exact action, identity,

46
00:04:41.091 --> 00:04:47.909
contract version, and expiry. Do not treat
a general conversation,

47
00:04:47.909 --> 00:04:54.727
earlier approval, or approval for one
target as permission for

48
00:04:54.727 --> 00:05:01.545
another. If arguments change after review,
request a new approval.

49
00:05:01.545 --> 00:05:08.364
Low-risk read or local draft operations
may be preauthorized by

50
00:05:08.364 --> 00:05:15.182
policy, but consequential effects need an
explicit rule. The application,

51
00:05:15.182 --> 00:05:22.000
not the prompt, decides whether that rule
is satisfied.

52
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.250
Design duplicate prevention before
enabling retries. Give each logical

53
00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:34.500
operation a caller-stable idempotency key.
Store the key with the

54
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:40.750
resolved target, operation, request
digest, and result. Reusing the key

55
00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:47.000
for different arguments is a conflict. A
timeout after a

56
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:53.250
write is not confirmed failure. Mark the
outcome unknown, query

57
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:59.500
the authoritative system, and reconcile
the idempotency record before any

58
00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:05.750
retry. If the effect occurred, return the
prior result. If

59
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:12.000
it definitely did not, a bounded retry may
be allowed.

60
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.250
If evidence remains ambiguous, stop and
escalate. Blind retry can

61
00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:24.500
duplicate messages, charges, permission
changes, or deletions. Compensation and

62
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:30.750
rollback are new actions with their own
authorization and failure

63
00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:37.000
evidence.

64
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:43.818
Use a trusted executor between model and
implementation. It authenticates

65
00:06:43.818 --> 00:06:50.636
the caller and workload, parses the
proposed call, validates schema

66
00:06:50.636 --> 00:06:57.455
and business meaning, resolves the target,
authorizes object and operation,

67
00:06:57.455 --> 00:07:04.273
verifies exact approval, enforces rate,
cost, concurrency, and time limits,

68
00:07:04.273 --> 00:07:11.091
reserves idempotency, executes, checks the
real postcondition, records a secret-safe

69
00:07:11.091 --> 00:07:17.909
audit event, and returns a stable result.
Treat tool output

70
00:07:17.909 --> 00:07:24.727
as untrusted data because it may be stale,
malformed, compromised,

71
00:07:24.727 --> 00:07:31.545
or contain instructions. Preserve call and
result linkage without placing

72
00:07:31.545 --> 00:07:38.364
secrets or unnecessary personal data into
traces. The executor can

73
00:07:38.364 --> 00:07:45.182
deny an action even when the model
strongly recommends it.

74
00:07:45.182 --> 00:07:52.000
That is the boundary doing its job.

75
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:59.000
Choose one consequential tool. Write one
schema constraint, one semantic

76
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:06.000
validation, one object-level authorization
check, one exact approval field, one

77
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:13.000
idempotency rule, and one independent
postcondition.

78
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:49.250
Checkpoint. A request matches the tool's
JSON schema and names

79
00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:55.500
an existing document, but the
authenticated caller does not own

80
00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:01.750
that document. The model says the change
is necessary. Which

81
00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:08.000
control decides the result?

82
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.000
Reject the action at object-level
authorization. Schema validity proves only

83
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:18.000
that the request can be parsed. The
document's existence proves

84
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.000
only that resolution succeeded. Neither
grants this caller permission. The

85
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:28.000
model's recommendation is not authority.
Record a denied decision without

86
00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.000
exposing the other workspace's sensitive
details. If you chose schema

87
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.000
validation, add the missing identity and
resource authorization step. If

88
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:43.000
you chose human approval, remember that
approval cannot grant a

89
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:48.000
reviewer authority they do not possess.

90
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.333
Open the typed-tool threat test. Define
one consequential tool, its

91
00:11:54.333 --> 00:12:00.667
stable result union, identity, validation,
authorization, exact approval, limits,

92
00:12:00.667 --> 00:12:07.000
idempotency, postcondition, audit, and
recovery controls. Run malformed

93
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:13.333
arguments, an unauthorized target,
injected tool output, timeout after

94
00:12:13.333 --> 00:12:19.667
possible success, and a duplicate call.
Retain the contract and

95
00:12:19.667 --> 00:12:26.000
five-case result table.

96
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.400
When ready, begin the knowledge check. You
will choose least

97
00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:34.800
privilege, place approval before impact,
distinguish schema from authorization,

98
00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:39.200
reconcile an uncertain write, and classify
tool output as untrusted

99
00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:43.600
data. The assessment begins only when you
choose Begin knowledge

100
00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.000
check.

101
00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:54.000
Let the model propose. Let trusted code
authenticate, validate, authorize,

102
00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:00.000
approve, execute once, verify, audit, and
recover.
